    <div id="hardware-problem" class="content-card">
      <h2>Problem Statement</h2>

      <h3 id="hardware-problem-symptoms">Waiting for someone to run a fever</h3>
      <p>Influenza A virus (IAV) is a persistent global public-health threat. WHO estimates that seasonal influenza causes around 1 billion infections, 3–5 million cases of severe illness and 290,000–650,000 respiratory deaths each year, with children, older adults and immunocompromised people carrying the heaviest burden <a class="hardware-cite" href="#hardware-reference-1">[1]</a>. The surface haemagglutinin (HA) protein continually escapes existing population immunity through antigenic drift and shift, so vaccine strains always lag behind circulating strains: the virus keeps changing, while our window on it opens only once symptoms appear.</p>
      <p>Starting from symptoms decides what such a chain can and cannot record. A throat swab can only be taken from someone already feverish or coughing; before that point, the particles they exhale are already in the air of the same room. This is not speculation: air-sampling studies have recovered influenza A virus RNA from indoor air in a health centre and a day-care centre, at total concentrations of 5.8×10³–3.7×10⁴ genome copies per cubic metre, with about 64% of the viral load carried on fine particles below 2.5 µm that can stay suspended for hours <a class="hardware-cite" href="#hardware-reference-2">[2]</a>. Patient-centred surveillance therefore carries a built-in delay — it records what has happened, and does not announce what is about to.</p>

      <h3 id="hardware-problem-air">The half that travels through the air</h3>
      <p>Bringing air into the picture is what closes that gap. The most direct gain is pre-symptomatic warning: detecting virus in indoor air before large numbers of clinical cases appear gives control measures a head start — ventilation, disinfection and crowd management can be scheduled before infections happen rather than after.</p>
      <p>It also puts risk in its proper place. Within one building, classrooms, wards and waiting halls do not carry the same risk, and air monitoring can tell which zones are the hot spots, so that limited effort goes where it is needed. For national influenza networks, built around clinical patients, this adds the environmental dimension that trend assessment currently lacks. And in the settings that carry the heaviest burden — schools and care homes — a real-time air risk assessment bears directly on reducing cluster outbreaks.</p>

      <h3 id="hardware-problem-dilution">Diluted almost to the point of disappearing</h3>
      <p>Finding virus in air is not primarily a question of whether it can be recognised, but of whether enough sample can be obtained. Viral particles occur at very low concentration in ambient air, and a sampler can process only a limited volume of it; multiply the two and what reaches the liquid is a very small amount indeed.</p>
      <p>The accepted paradigm is a two-step workflow: a bioaerosol sampler collects airborne particles on site, and the sample is then sent back to a central laboratory for qRT-PCR. It is sensitive and dependable, but it splits sampling from detection across two places and two moments, with transport and laboratory turnaround in between, so what comes back is usually retrospective data rather than a field result <a class="hardware-cite" href="#hardware-reference-3">[3]</a>. Sampling itself also loses material: the collection efficiency of cyclone samplers varies widely with design and falls at high flow rates as the collection liquid evaporates and already-captured particles are re-aerosolised <a class="hardware-cite" href="#hardware-reference-4">[4]</a>. No mature low-cost option exists for on-site airborne IAV detection — that gap is what we set out to fill.</p>

      <h3 id="hardware-problem-signal">Choosing what emits the signal</h3>
      <p>The next question is what should emit the signal. The familiar answer is a chemical reagent: a chromogenic substrate, an antibody, an enzyme label. These are mature and reproducible, but under conditions that here hold at the same time — airborne IAV, on site, low cost, continuous — each of them is constrained in its own way.</p>
