    <div id="hardware-instrument" class="content-card">
      <h2>The Complete Instrument and the Overall Plan</h2>
      <p>Beyond the modules, we also have a full set of technical requirements for the instrument as a whole: a wet-wall cyclone bioaerosol sampling and optical detection instrument that integrates cyclone sampling, a COC collection cell, temperature control, optical detection, data display and host software in a single machine. This is not a superseded earlier concept — it is our picture of the final product, and it is the basis on which the current modules were designed: their interfaces, dimensions and specifications were all set to meet the requirements of the complete instrument.</p>
      <p>The overall plan does include a display and host software. An instrument sitting in a classroom or a hospital ward has to show temperature and fluorescence readings without a computer attached to it, and it has to be able to export continuous data for trend analysis. Our order of work is to build the core functions first — temperature control, optics and the signal chain — and to make those three stable, repeatable and reproducible before layering display and host software on top. The current hardware revision covers the core functions, with the module interfaces already reserved for that later expansion.</p>
