    <div id="hardware-challenges" class="content-card">
      <h2>Challenges and Lessons</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Print settings and supports: the failed first print showed that print orientation and support placement must be considered at the design stage — long slender cones string badly.</li>
        <li>Channel size: only after widening the optical channel to 8 mm did it become usable. That is the lower bound that still fits Ø10 mm filters, a lens assembly and alignment margin.</li>
        <li>Detachable modules: making the optics plug-in moves the alignment problem from the assembled instrument into the plug itself. Once the plug is fixed, assembling the instrument becomes a repeatable insertion.</li>
        <li>Co-culture cuts both ways: putting the yeast directly into the sampling liquid overlaps sampling time with expression time, but it also brings foaming, evaporation, contamination and a moving liquid surface into the optical path. Each of these has to be quantified during testing.</li>
        <li>Enrichment is the bottleneck: conversations with experts made clear that target virus concentrations in air are extremely low, and that enrichment efficiency, more than detection sensitivity, limits the whole system. This is why we kept the cyclone sampling and liquid-enrichment route.</li>
        <li>Validate before integrating: running temperature control and optics with a standard cuvette first keeps the uncertainty of a custom vessel out of optical debugging.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div id="hardware-future" class="content-card">
      <h2>Future Work</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Integrate the cyclone sampling front end with the heated block so that sampling, enrichment and detection close the loop in one cup, and quantify the enrichment efficiency.</li>
        <li>Verify yeast viability and readout stability under co-culture, and fix the sampling flow rate, sampling duration and settling time.</li>
        <li>Replace the PLA parts with the CNC parts once they arrive, and re-check window parallelism, surface quality and light-tightness.</li>
        <li>Complete the first integrated test to establish detection limit, repeatability and response time.</li>
        <li>Once the core functions are validated, add the data display and host software in line with the overall plan, and complete the full instrument specification.</li>
      </ul>
